      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Type 2 diabetes is diagnosed using specific blood tests that measure your blood sugar or average glucose levels. MedlinePlus (.gov)",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Diagnostic Blood Tests"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Hemoglobin A1C Test: Measures your average blood sugar level over the past 2 to 3 months.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diabetes: 6.5% or higher"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Prediabetes: 5.7% to 6.4% Cleveland Clinic +1",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1,
                  2
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Fasting Plasma Glucose (FPG) Test: Measures your blood sugar after you fast (not eating or drinking anything except water) for at least 8 hours.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diabetes: 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L) or higher"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Prediabetes: 100 to 125 mg/dL (5.6 to 6.9 mmol/L) MedlinePlus (.gov) +2",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  1,
                  2
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT): Measures your blood sugar before and 2 hours after you drink a special sugary liquid.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diabetes: 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L) or higher at the 2-hour mark"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Prediabetes: 140 to 199 mg/dL (7.8 to 11.0 mmol/L) Centers for Disease Control and Prevention | CDC (.gov) +2",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  2,
                  3,
                  4
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Random Plasma Glucose Test: Measures your blood sugar at any time of day.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Diabetes: 200 mg/dL or higher, especially if you also have classic symptoms like extreme thirst, frequent urination, or unexplained weight loss. Mayo Clinic +1",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1,
                  5
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Confirmation and Screening"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Repeat Testing: In the absence of very high blood sugar or clear, classic symptoms, doctors usually require a second test (either on a separate day or a repeat of the same test) to confirm the diagnosis. American Diabetes Association +1",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Routine Screening: The American Diabetes Association recommends routine screening for all adults starting at age 35, or earlier if you have risk factors such as overweight or obesity, a family history of diabetes, high blood pressure, or a history of gestational diabetes. Mayo Clinic +1",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "American Diabetes Association",
                "link": "https://diabetes.org/about-diabetes/diagnosis"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Are you asking for yourself or someone else? If you'd like to share any symptoms or recent lab numbers, I can help explain what they generally mean."
      }
